b9d4b0557d75a9a57d51e583a8d32c91ff63dcd67c9700fc2" "q-130166/selection/42c8d2f51ae38f7e92e407da31a06453bbf6a4aae7d6f1aafaf931ebae806998"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130166" :question/id #uuid "8c02a646-368e-54d5-8b44-55a70a155c58" :question/math-academy-id "q-130166" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+18y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+18{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +18) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +18=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +18 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+18 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-130166/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2999a565-bf64-5246-9fee-54cecc9e79ff"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130166"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130335/selection/88eb9fabf5da9f2f91b509c02e56f2d8a0bbf2f911adc36fdf75bc0de6cb4721" :answer/id #uuid "2ca9e62d-0af7-5c29-9226-eb356e9afeef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130335/selection/33509e3ec856f16adb85a0a3499c24cbf6b5ad70cc5009fe78386e6c371ac667" :answer/id #uuid "4bf87abe-9d5a-5383-b473-2af663f38bfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130335/selection/6213ded15c8443faf20558eeadd1a4fc5078d7d44c91ed8b4266356ba5d9000a" :answer/id #uuid "c864e5a9-6ac3-5e5f-8ae4-386ed10351cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130335/selection/fb9c44ff249b05b9e68098083847081fdafea153ce25d334811000fa66ab3d1f" :answer/id #uuid "1221dd3c-a862-5432-bae7-0238cf18ca17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130335/selection/ba37166b0ebff64a9e22ea46f6425bcaac29ea4a9ea8107d2fb12db90b62a3c6" :answer/id #uuid "4ebd7184-cb3b-509b-b2cd-8527807e152e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130335/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3702d517-03e9-51ad-91a2-bb0914344831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-130335/selection/88eb9fabf5da9f2f91b509c02e56f2d8a0bbf2f911adc36fdf75bc0de6cb4721" :answer-field/choices ["q-130335/selection/88eb9fabf5da9f2f91b509c02e56f2d8a0bbf2f911adc36fdf75bc0de6cb4721" "q-130335/selection/33509e3ec856f16adb85a0a3499c24cbf6b5ad70cc5009fe78386e6c371ac667" "q-130335/selection/6213ded15c8443faf20558eeadd1a4fc5078d7d44c91ed8b4266356ba5d9000a" "q-130335/selection/fb9c44ff249b05b9e68098083847081fdafea153ce25d334811000fa66ab3d1f" "q-130335/selection/ba37166b0ebff64a9e22ea46f6425bcaac29ea4a9ea8107d2fb12db90b62a3c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130335" :question/id #uuid "af9af2fb-ef1d-5340-9a94-3dfeb8f038e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-130335" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}$ are the purely imaginary roots of the auxiliary equation. \n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-130335/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130335"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-64698/selection/62eb584f3cf197941b35a0dff32a4b55b0317ec34132783fe620a24c1d0b53f1" :answer/id #uuid "81e843c0-2278-5088-8eba-d4a2a4dbc88d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64698/selection/a7a3a77d9e348e7c09cf909d93ae3113aa889d526924bfa6c8c6b4f61c2b9519" :answer/id #uuid "7b8d1915-a7fd-5489-8914-243896b047b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64698/selection/4c555d478bd45c97081b255643d21b017c791c9e8e9159e4de6cc49e1103fdd8" :answer/id #uuid "abe76ce1-2c80-57bd-bc3f-312fe1453871" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64698/selection/353f856da92be80b3deed2cf4396b4faa7e710a0439b10bca33f2f0c01e4b0d4" :answer/id #uuid "95098154-8f31-53ab-a102-dfe883e218ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64698/selection/644929609f92d83270d13ce3498c0e84bef007f1bc93e76c96722a829d95ba81" :answer/id #uuid "c61a220f-9eca-5631-8111-7374e0165e2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64698/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1bc56112-e2d2-5ed1-921b-6b14da3226b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-64698/selection/644929609f92d83270d13ce3498c0e84bef007f1bc93e76c96722a829d95ba81" :answer-field/choices ["q-64698/selection/62eb584f3cf197941b35a0dff32a4b55b0317ec34132783fe620a24c1d0b53f1" "q-64698/selection/a7a3a77d9e348e7c09cf909d93ae3113aa889d526924bfa6c8c6b4f61c2b9519" "q-64698/selection/4c555d478bd45c97081b255643d21b017c791c9e8e9159e4de6cc49e1103fdd8" "q-64698/selection/353f856da92be80b3deed2cf4396b4faa7e710a0439b10bca33f2f0c01e4b0d4" "q-64698/selection/644929609f92d83270d13ce3498c0e84bef007f1bc93e76c96722a829d95ba81"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-64698" :question/id #uuid "fd53d7ed-c933-5340-a31b-528b4220b307" :question/math-academy-id "q-64698" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+64y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+64{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+64) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+64=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-64 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 8\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda =\\pm 8\\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation. \n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{8\\text{i}t})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{8\\text{i}t})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-64698/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64698"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130336/selection/ee811651d7ebdd5662d32b0f6c0bf57f2c6871d58471450812a73af0b19bff69" :answer/id #uuid "5199578a-42f1-512b-8325-66389d799f80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130336/selection/325f073c446dd76f2d8232f4dc6cf0a05ab21cbda084f3f10ed041a9548c13cf" :answer/id #uuid "1dd674c1-7e58-5df5-8724-dade248e1abb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130336/selection/430893a42c5e6c8e377c09f410c5b1ddc6b3d767a006e792a89244db7510824e" :answer/id #uuid "57cc03b7-229a-5822-afca-9e9d042e21d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130336/selection/beea7ec528deb07edf27795781e8490db51fa602bbf0bf5f158d57307bf446d3" :answer/id #uuid "0661a131-9355-5bf6-b326-5be225efd2ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130336/selection/0d76add1b1cecb96dc7e904bc9abf1e8fcbc79fdf56de23cd7c582b21a8f5bf9" :answer/id #uuid "b00595dc-961b-598a-a7e6-4f92d750d335" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130336/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e08d00b-fa0e-5a02-af64-ebee6d207146" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-130336/selection/beea7ec528deb07edf27795781e8490db51fa602bbf0bf5f158d57307bf446d3" :answer-field/choices ["q-130336/selection/ee811651d7ebdd5662d32b0f6c0bf57f2c6871d58471450812a73af0b19bff69" "q-130336/selection/325f073c446dd76f2d8232f4dc6cf0a05ab21cbda084f3f10ed041a9548c13cf" "q-130336/selection/430893a42c5e6c8e377c09f410c5b1ddc6b3d767a006e792a89244db7510824e" "q-130336/selection/beea7ec528deb07edf27795781e8490db51fa602bbf0bf5f158d57307bf446d3" "q-130336/selection/0d76add1b1cecb96dc7e904bc9abf1e8fcbc79fdf56de23cd7c582b21a8f5bf9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130336" :question/id #uuid "48df2853-dfc5-51a7-b6a3-b25bc596c153" :question/math-academy-id "q-130336" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+17y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+17{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +17) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +17=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +17 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+17 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =-4\\pm \\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(-4+\\text{i})x})={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(-4+\\text{i})x})={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{x}$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-130336/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130336"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130330/selection/deb758912c2f729830dac7fa561027ddd7e87f10d332961af8d121f56554e2c4" :answer/id #uuid "b34084c6-bdc4-5670-a829-1731b4b7b235" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130330/selection/fcd8ef74d1bad99f5267c45021b27d4a0dec02bc2e0797de7d7f2602cab3d362" :answer/id #uuid "fba5e3e8-052e-5cd3-ac56-eb8e822c8d9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130330/selection/ae6efd6032a8653e41bc29a8c7939986d606f2c0b0c77a929a6eb94d8ab01c0a" :answer/id #uuid "4140ac64-3940-5efe-8ec2-71ab8a77b432" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130330/selection/df904ab44e0846a17a2370ea4a3f3a20aa4b872a1c598dbb50edc7e1137da231" :answer/id #uuid "40b9d118-62ca-5f90-bb95-34f158533c7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130330/selection/76bae7d77851501c56456607cdd735f1281a5a034d8338ab73654795ab0258d1" :answer/id #uuid "d677b3c7-fe6f-5d82-86e9-a0bebecd02a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130330/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8afba1a9-7b8b-5ff3-a33a-ad48325c4b1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-130330/selection/deb758912c2f729830dac7fa561027ddd7e87f10d332961af8d121f56554e2c4" :answer-field/choices ["q-130330/selection/deb758912c2f729830dac7fa561027ddd7e87f10d332961af8d121f56554e2c4" "q-130330/selection/fcd8ef74d1bad99f5267c45021b27d4a0dec02bc2e0797de7d7f2602cab3d362" "q-130330/selection/ae6efd6032a8653e41bc29a8c7939986d606f2c0b0c77a929a6eb94d8ab01c0a" "q-130330/selection/df904ab44e0846a17a2370ea4a3f3a20aa4b872a1c598dbb50edc7e1137da231" "q-130330/selection/76bae7d77851501c56456607cdd735f1281a5a034d8338ab73654795ab0258d1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130330" :question/id #uuid "d21543bf-bb06-541c-a58e-8a0367707d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130330" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+50y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+50{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +50) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +50=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +50 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+50 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+49 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-49 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 7\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 7\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =-1\\pm 7\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(-1+7\\text{i})x})={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(-1+7\\text{i})x})={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $50{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-130330/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130330"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-90807/selection/d239a92f98e9cda20266ee68feba2d2de7c946f8b95b7df3f1cd8c5e03a08a17" :answer/id #uuid "29d2b3b0-e7d7-5c6a-8b16-e19b7584117a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90807/selection/cd3aeff07bcee3ef6a6c26f3ad3fa22db20fa76cfb7e9cee186b61c07e3b6038" :answer/id #uuid "9bbdc9d8-130f-53bb-ac6b-7e6bb0cb84e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90807/selection/96f8cb0152177e3c54f752692aebca465ae28b8e1eefd7019a340f753730a5c5" :answer/id #uuid "add85793-6d23-5282-89c7-86a95f8bbd06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90807/selection/fa51e7d8bb19cd024d4c5947cc669d537d58a560175c58c19faad0326267bd16" :answer/id #uuid "68c8dfac-316b-5387-9deb-cf23ca987b04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90807/selection/181dc5c0c937fd064aaf44e152c088e866b4ba3e80f1026b43c776841201a435" :answer/id #uuid "5907155d-3351-5525-8d1d-4ad2fb69ecf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90807/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7e5570-66be-50e3-8e45-552f173a26f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-90807/selection/cd3aeff07bcee3ef6a6c26f3ad3fa22db20fa76cfb7e9cee186b61c07e3b6038" :answer-field/choices ["q-90807/selection/d239a92f98e9cda20266ee68feba2d2de7c946f8b95b7df3f1cd8c5e03a08a17" "q-90807/selection/cd3aeff07bcee3ef6a6c26f3ad3fa22db20fa76cfb7e9cee186b61c07e3b6038" "q-90807/selection/96f8cb0152177e3c54f752692aebca465ae28b8e1eefd7019a340f753730a5c5" "q-90807/selection/fa51e7d8bb19cd024d4c5947cc669d537d58a560175c58c19faad0326267bd16" "q-90807/selection/181dc5c0c937fd064aaf44e152c088e866b4ba3e80f1026b43c776841201a435"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-90807" :question/id #uuid "d12a760d-b095-5ba3-95f1-2e581862de00" :question/math-academy-id "q-90807" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-12{y}^{′}+40y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+40{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36)-36+40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -6 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =6\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =6\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-90807/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-90807"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6595"] :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 25y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x}  = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2}  = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+25{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+25) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny = P\\cos{5x} + Q\\sin{5x}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6597"] :question/problem "Consider the equation $y'' + 10y' + 34y  = 0,$ where $y = y(t).$ If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+34{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +34) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 34  = 0,\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +34 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25)-25+34 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +5 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda = - 5 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6598"] :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 4 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 20y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x}  = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2}  = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our ODE gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+20{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20 & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6596"] :question/problem "Given that $y = y(t)$ satisfies the equation $y'' - 4y' + 85y = 0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n- $-2e^{2t}\\cos{9t} + 7e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$\n\n- $e^{2t}\\cos{9t}+3e^{2t}\\sin{3t}$\n\n- $-6e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+85{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 85 = 0,\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+85 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-81 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 9\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\lambda = 2 \\pm 9 \\textrm{i}$ are the complex roots of the characteristic equation, the general solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny = e^{2t} \\left( P \\cos 9t + Q \\sin 9t \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $P=-2$ and $Q=7.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II does *not* take the above form. It contains $\\sin 3t,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III takes the above form with $P=0$ and $Q=-6.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
